{
  "0": "Across all four datasets, add a residual feed-forward block after each GRU output: h'=h+W2(GELU(W1(h))), with intermediate width 256 and W2 initialized to zero. Apply it identically in training and inference. This preserves the initial recurrent representation while allowing nonlinear refinement for item compatibility. Keep embeddings, history selection, final-real-position readout, dot-product scoring, loss and optimizer fixed. Update the predictor writer to load the added parameters; retain one model per category.",
  "1": "Across all four datasets, truncate observed histories to the latest 32 items instead of 128 for both training and inference, retaining left padding and final-real-position readout. Most histories fit within 32 items; removing older context from longer histories may reduce stale-interest interference without the state averaging that regressed previously. Keep embeddings, GRU topology, scoring, loss, optimizer and training schedule fixed, and save maxlen=32 in predictor metadata.",
  "2": "Across all four datasets, reduce the pointwise coefficient from 0.2 to 0.05: softplus(negative-positive)+0.05*[softplus(-positive)+softplus(negative)], averaged uniformly over existing nonpadding targets. The successful mixed objective motivates retaining score anchoring while testing whether weaker absolute-score pressure improves ranking. Preserve embeddings, GRU topology, readout, scoring, negative sampling, optimizer and training settings; inference remains unchanged.",
  "3": "Across all four datasets, change tied item embedding outputs from unit norm to fixed norm 1.25, retaining width 128 and exact zero padding. The norm-0.75 trial regressed; testing modestly larger magnitudes may strengthen recurrent input signals and candidate-score separation under the incumbent mixed objective. This is an embedding-only hypothesis, not evidence that larger norms must improve performance. Preserve GRU topology, history policy, readout, scoring formula, loss and optimizer; use identical normalization in the predictor."
}
